Z||000000||HummelHund||09-23-2001||11:34 PM||||84 4 cyl with oil globs in coolant bottle and radiator, but none apparent on dispstick. Any ideas as to how to determine what exactly is wrong? Also, can a techie explain how oil gets forced into the water? I thought it was the other way around.<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||Posthumane||09-23-2001||11:52 PM||||Is your's an automatic? usually if there's oil in the coolant, and not the other way around, it's a leak in the oil cooler from the tranny (inside the rad). If it's a standard, then I don't know what it could be. If it was a problem with the head gasket, you would have compression blowing by into the coolant (bubbles). ||||reg||1
Z||000002||HummelHund||09-24-2001||12:56 AM||||I forgot to mention it's a standard. I agree if it was an auto I'd be looking that way - BTDT, but it's not. We recently went from synthetic (only one change for maybe 500 miles to clean it out) back to dino oil, anything wierd with that that would cause oil in water??||||reg||5
Z||000003||Posthumane||09-24-2001||01:02 AM||||I don't see why it would, although I just switched to synth, and it seems to leak more that it used to. I think synthetic oil gets into smaller cracks than dino oil, because it doesn't have as much sludge in it, so if you have a small crack somewhere that would cause oil to go into the water, it may be more of a problem with synth.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Darren's 87 coupe||09-24-2001||02:22 AM||||hey man,<BR>I'm in the same boat as you.  I have posted this same question and am not getting much in the way of answers either.  Can you possibly message me with the solution once you get it figured out?  i am not going to get to work on mine for a few months and if you have yours figured out by then i would sure appriciate a note to help me out.  I have an 87 5 speed 4cyl.  Same exact problem<BR>Thanks<BR>Darren||||reg||1
Z||000005||HummelHund||09-24-2001||10:14 AM||||I should also mention the car does not overheat. Only time it got hot at all was in April when the alt belt broke. We had it towed home and it has run fine ever since with no hint of a problem with that. Maybe I should flush the coolant and see what happens then.??? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||09-24-2001||11:40 AM||||In the crancase and in the cyls, there is always an oil/air vapor or mist. Even in the combustion area. In an engine with good rings, it is too small to notice, in other words, no blue smoke in the exh. If you have a crack somewhere, the pressure in the combustion area will force this oil vapor into the coolant stream. When the coolant cools off, the vapor will condense into gobules. Sometimes, the crack is so small, that it only opens when the metal of the engine is heated up, allowing the oil vapor to be forced into the coolant. Any coolant that enters the combustion area probably turns to steam. Coolant usually enters the oil area when the engine is shut down, unless the crack is in an unpressurized area. Even then, the coolant may boil off due to the high temp of the oil and surrounding metal, and you will hardly notice it. Do you see any indication of moisture on the underside of the oil fill cap in the mornings when the eng is cold? Coolant will often condsense there, or on the underside of the valve cover.I use the word crack; it could be a gasket, any leak.<BR>I have not run across this on my 84 4cyl, but worked on diesels for years. What you describe was a common occurance in '71 series diesels and large bore Cats. There is another remote possibility that I can think of. Water pumps have lubricant in the bearings and sometimes it finds it's way into the coolant, but this usually only happens with a newly installed pump. Hope this makes sense. Good luck. <BR>||||reg||1
